WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.218
This is STE-80. It is a writing style for technical text.

2
00:00:04.316 --> 00:00:09.412
It uses about eighty percent of the rules of ASD-STE100, Simplified

3
00:00:09.512 --> 00:00:14.380
Technical English, a standard for aircraft maintenance manuals.

4
00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:18.436
Manuals are read under time pressure, often in a second language, so

5
00:00:18.536 --> 00:00:19.670
every word must help.

6
00:00:20.766 --> 00:00:24.689
Here is a typical sentence from a manual. It has sixteen words:

7
00:00:24.789 --> 00:00:28.342
the orange bar counts them. Some of the words do not help the reader.

8
00:00:28.433 --> 00:00:32.903
Rule one. Use simple words. The words in red go.

9
00:00:33.003 --> 00:00:36.504
Replenished becomes full. Prior to becomes before.

10
00:00:36.604 --> 00:00:38.390
Commencing becomes starting.

11
00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:41.654
Only one word goes, but each word is easier to read.

12
00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:45.410
Rule two. Give the instruction directly.

13
00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:49.836
Remove the frame, it is imperative that the operator ensures, and tell

14
00:00:49.936 --> 00:00:54.250
the reader what to do: make sure, before you start. Thirteen words.

15
00:00:55.166 --> 00:00:58.028
Rule three. Use a concrete verb.

16
00:00:58.128 --> 00:01:01.556
Make sure that it is full asks the reader to check a result.

17
00:01:01.656 --> 00:01:03.549
The verb fill names the action.

18
00:01:03.649 --> 00:01:07.227
And the vague noun operation becomes the machine you start.

19
00:01:07.327 --> 00:01:08.272
Nine words.

20
00:01:09.266 --> 00:01:11.180
Sixteen words became nine.

21
00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:15.030
The reader still fills the reservoir before starting the machine, with

22
00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:17.800
less work to read it. Full STE has more rules:

23
00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:21.962
short sentences, one meaning per word, and an approved dictionary.
